TITLE:: RLPitchSystem
summary:: A pitch system: its pitch classes, its pitches, and how they sound
categories:: Libraries>reLation
related:: Classes/RLEDO, Classes/RLJust, Classes/RLVoiceLeading, Guides/reLation

DESCRIPTION::
The abstract class of link::Classes/RLEDO:: (an equal division of the octave) and link::Classes/RLJust:: (just intonation as a lattice). A strong::pitch class:: is a note once octaves are forgotten, a strong::pitch:: is a note. Each system has its own coordinates for both, plain Integers and Arrays, and answers the same questions about them:

table::
## system || pitch class || pitch
## code::RLEDO(19):: || an Integer, 0..18 || an Integer, steps from the origin
## code::RLJust([3, 5]):: || code::[e3, e5]::, exponents of 3 and 5 || code::[e2, e3, e5]::, the exponent of 2 first
::

Pitch classes form a group (link::#-pcZero::, link::#-pcAdd::, link::#-pcNeg::, link::#-pcSub::, link::#-pcScale::): an interval between two pitch classes is a pitch class too. Pitches lie on a line: link::#-height:: is a pitch's place on it in octaves above the origin, link::#-nearest:: the pitch of a class closest to a height. code::interval([5, 4]):: is the system's pitch class for a just ratio: exact in RLJust, the nearest step in RLEDO.

A system is a value: two systems with the same numbers are equal and hash alike.

code::
~edo = RLEDO(12);  ~just = RLJust([3, 5]);
~edo.interval([3, 2]);                   // 7
~just.interval([5, 3]);                  // [-1, 1]: a fifth down, a major third up
~just.cents(~just.interval([81, 80]));   // 21.5
~just.temperPc(~edo, [4, -1]);           // 0: 12 steps temper the syntonic comma out
::

CLASSMETHODS::

METHOD:: factor
The prime factors of a positive Integer, a Dictionary prime -> exponent.

METHOD:: monzo
A ratio as prime exponents, a Dictionary prime -> signed exponent: code::[5, 4]:: gives code::(5: 1, 2: -2)::.
argument:: ratio
An Array code::[numerator, denominator]::, or a number (read as the nearest simple fraction).

INSTANCEMETHODS::

METHOD:: pcZero
The pitch class of the origin.

METHOD:: pcAdd
METHOD:: pcSub
METHOD:: pcNeg
METHOD:: pcScale
The group of pitch classes: sum, difference, opposite, and a pitch class taken k times.

METHOD:: pc
The class of a pitch.

METHOD:: pcHeight
A pitch class's place in the octave, in [0, 1).

METHOD:: height
A pitch's place in octaves above the origin.

METHOD:: nearest
The pitch of a class closest to a height (in octaves).

METHOD:: above
The pitch of a class at or above a height, within an octave.

METHOD:: interval
The pitch class of a just ratio (code::[3, 2]::, or a number).

METHOD:: pitchAdd
METHOD:: pitchSub
METHOD:: pitchNeg
Sum, difference and opposite of pitches (of intervals between pitches).

METHOD:: octave
The octave as an interval between pitches.

METHOD:: cents
The size of a pitch-class interval in cents, the shorter way round: in (-600, 600]. A fifth is -500 (a fourth down).

METHOD:: pitchCents
The size of an interval between pitches, in cents.

METHOD:: ratio
The frequency ratio of a pitch to the origin.

METHOD:: freq
The frequency of a pitch.
argument:: pitch
argument:: base
The frequency of the origin (middle C by default).

METHOD:: name
A pitch class as a String: note names in 12 steps, step numbers in other divisions, a ratio on the lattice.

METHOD:: isFinite
Whether the system has finitely many pitch classes.
